{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Triana. Population around 500. A small, historic community sitting right on the Tennessee River where southwest Madison County meets the water. This is one of Alabama's oldest settlements, with roots going back to the early 1800s and a story that's woven into the region's history.</p>

<p>Living on the river comes with trade-offs. The views are beautiful. The fishing is good. The water table is high. That last part matters when you're talking about drainage.</p>

<p><strong>The water table reality:</strong> In Triana, groundwater sits close to the surface. Your foundation isn't just dealing with surface runoff — it's dealing with pressure from below. When your gutters fail and roof water dumps next to the foundation, you're adding to an already challenging situation. Proper gutter drainage is essential here.</p>

<p><strong>Humidity effects:</strong> River humidity accelerates wear on everything. Metal corrodes faster, wood rots sooner, and maintenance cycles shorten. We recommend aluminum gutters for most river-area homes because they handle humidity better than steel alternatives.</p>

<p><strong>Historic home considerations:</strong> Many <a href="/recent-projects/">Triana homes</a> have been here for decades, some much longer. These homes often have irregular rooflines, non-standard fascia, and drainage systems that were added piecemeal over the years. We have experience working with older construction and can design gutter systems that fit the character of historic homes.</p>

<p><strong>Flood awareness:</strong> River communities live with flood risk. While gutters can't protect against major flooding, proper drainage keeps normal rainfall from compounding problems. Every bit of water routed away from your foundation helps.</p>

<p>We serve Triana regularly and understand what river-area homes need. No guesswork, just solutions designed for your actual conditions.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Practical advice for Triana homeowners:</p>

<p><strong>Bigger is better for gutters:</strong> Standard 5-inch gutters can get overwhelmed during heavy storms. Consider 6-inch gutters with 3x4 downspouts for better capacity. The extra flow rate matters when water is coming down hard.</p>

<p><strong>Underground drains are worth considering:</strong> With high water tables, you want roof runoff going far away from your foundation — not just a few feet. Underground drains route water 20-50+ feet away where it can't add to groundwater pressure near your home.</p>

<p><strong>Material matters:</strong> Choose corrosion-resistant materials. Aluminum handles river humidity better than steel. Quality hangers and fasteners resist rust. Spending a bit more upfront saves money long-term.</p>

<p><strong>Watch for humidity damage:</strong> Check gutters and fascia regularly for early signs of corrosion, rot, or deterioration. River-area homes need more frequent inspection than properties farther inland.</p>

<p><strong>Seasonal timing:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>March:</strong> Pre-storm season check — major rain events hit hardest in spring</li>
<li><strong>November:</strong> Clear debris before winter, check for summer humidity damage</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Signs your system needs attention:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Water pooling near foundation after rain</li>
<li>Gutters overflowing during moderate storms</li>
<li>Rust or corrosion on metal components</li>
<li>Soft spots or discoloration on fascia</li>
<li>Basement moisture after rainfall</li>
</ul>

